const READINGS = {
1:[
"My name is Jim Hawkins, and Doctor Livesey and Squire Trelawney have asked me to write down everything I remember about Treasure Island — everything except the exact position of the island, because there is still treasure on it.",
"When I was a boy I lived with my father and mother at the Admiral Benbow Inn, a lonely house on the cliffs above a small cove. Ships passed but they did not stop, and for most of the year the only sound was the sea.",
"Then, one winter morning, an old sailor came up the road with a wheelbarrow behind him. He was tall and heavy, his hands were hard and marked, and there was a long dirty scar down one cheek. He looked at the cove for a long time, and then he sang, half to himself, a song I would hear a hundred times before the end:",
"“Fifteen men on the dead man’s chest — Yo-ho-ho and a bottle of rum!”",
"He asked my father for a glass of rum and drank it slowly, still looking at the water.",
"“Do many people come here?” he asked.",
"“No,” said my father. “Very few.”",
"“Then this is the place for me. I’ll stay. You can call me captain. Bacon and eggs, and rum, and a room where I can watch the ships.” He put some gold coins on the table and said nothing else all afternoon.",
"He stayed for weeks, and then for months. Every day he walked along the cliffs with a brass telescope under his arm. Every evening he sat in the corner of the room with his rum, and he watched the door.",
"One morning he took me aside and gave me a silver fourpenny piece.",
"“You shall have one of these on the first of every month,” he said, “if you keep your eyes open for a seafaring man with one leg — and tell me the moment you see him.”",
"He looked so frightened when he said it that I was frightened too. For months afterwards that man walked through my dreams. Sometimes he had one leg in the middle of his body; sometimes he was cut off at the hip; sometimes he chased me over the cliffs. I earned my fourpence in nightmares.",
"But it was the captain himself who frightened the neighbours. When he had been drinking he sang his songs and made everyone else sing with him, and the house shook. He told stories about hanging and drowning and wild deeds in the Caribbean, and the quiet country people listened with their eyes wide and came back the next night to listen again.",
"My father was sure the inn would be ruined. People would stop coming, he said; they would be frightened away. But I think it was the other way round — the fear was exciting, and it gave the village something to talk about. What it did to my father was worse. He grew quieter and thinner all that winter, and his cough got no better.",
"The captain never paid another penny after the first few weeks. He stayed on, week after week, and none of us dared to ask him for money.",
"Only one man in the village was not afraid of him, and that was Doctor Livesey.",
"He came one evening to see my father, and afterwards he sat at the table talking to old Mr Dance while he waited for his horse. The captain, very drunk, began to sing.",
"Everyone in the room went quiet, as they always did. Everyone except the doctor, who went on speaking calmly to his neighbour.",
"The captain banged the table with his hand. “Silence!” he shouted.",
"“Were you speaking to me, sir?” asked the doctor.",
"“I was.”",
"“Then I have only one thing to say to you,” said Doctor Livesey. “If you keep on drinking rum like that, the world will soon be rid of a very dirty scoundrel.”",
"The captain’s face went red. He pulled out a sailor’s knife and opened it in his hand.",
"Doctor Livesey did not move. He did not raise his voice either. He simply said, over his shoulder, “If you do not put that knife back in your pocket this instant, I promise you, upon my honour, that you shall hang at the next assizes. I am not only a doctor here, I am a magistrate; and if I hear one word against you, I shall have you hunted out of this country.”",
"For a long moment nobody breathed. Then the captain folded the knife and put it away and sat down like a beaten dog. And I understood something that evening which I have never forgotten: that there is more than one kind of power in the world, and that the quiet kind can be the stronger."
],
2:[
"It was a cold January morning when the captain went down to the beach with his telescope, as he did most days. My father was very ill upstairs, and my mother was with him.",
"A stranger came into the inn while he was out. He was pale, and there were two fingers missing from his left hand, and although he wore a cutlass he did not look like a fighting man.",
"“Is my old friend Bill here?” he asked.",
"“I don’t know anybody called Bill,” I said. “The captain is our only guest.”",
"“Ah,” said the man. “A tall man with a cut on one cheek, and a very pleasant way with him when he’s drunk. My friend Bill. Is he in?”",
"“He’s walking on the beach,” I said.",
"“Then we’ll wait for him,” said the stranger. He pulled me with him behind the door and made me stand there in the shadow, and I could feel him shaking. He kept his hand on the handle of his cutlass all the time.",
"When the captain came in and shut the door behind him, the stranger stepped out.",
"“Bill,” he said.",
"The captain turned round. All the colour went out of his face — even the scar looked white.",
"“Black Dog,” he said, in a voice like a man who has met a ghost.",
"They sat down together and talked in low voices for a long time, and I brought them rum and left them alone. Then the voices got louder, and there was shouting, and suddenly a crash of chairs and the noise of steel on steel.",
"Black Dog came running out with blood on his shoulder and disappeared down the road. The captain stood in the doorway with his cutlass in his hand, staring after him — and then he swayed, put his hand to his throat, and fell full length on the floor.",
"Doctor Livesey was in the house, and he came down at once. It was not a wound: it was the captain’s heart, and the rum.",
"“Mr Bones,” said the doctor, “if you take one more glass of rum, you will die. Do you understand me? One more glass.”",
"Between us we carried him up to bed. When we rolled up his sleeve to bleed him I saw a tattoo on his arm: BILLY BONES, HIS FANCY.",
"He lay there three days. On the third he called me to him and whispered: “Jim, they’ll give me the black spot. They want my old sea-chest. If they give me the black spot, you ride for that doctor of yours, and bring the whole lot of them — magistrates and all — and we’ll catch Flint’s old crew at the Admiral Benbow, every man of them.”",
"That night my father died. Between the funeral and the work of the inn I had no time to think about the captain’s fears, and he came downstairs again and sat in his corner, weaker, but drinking as much as ever.",
"The day after the funeral, a blind man came up the road.",
"He was tapping in front of him with a stick, and he wore a great old boat-cloak with a hood, and a green shade over his eyes. I have never seen a more dreadful figure.",
"“Will any kind friend tell a poor blind man where he is?” he said.",
"“You are at the Admiral Benbow,” I told him.",
"“I hear a voice,” he said, “a young voice. Will you give me your hand, my kind young friend, and lead me in?”",
"I held out my hand — and the soft, blind, smiling creature closed on it like a trap. I cried out and tried to pull away, and he pulled me close to him with terrible strength.",
"“Now, boy,” he said, “take me to the captain.”",
"He twisted my arm until I thought I would faint, and he made me lead him into the room.",
"The captain looked up, and the rum went out of his face at one look and left him staring sober and sick.",
"“Now, Bill,” said the blind man, “hold out your right hand.” He put something into the captain’s palm, closed his fingers over it, and let go of me at last. “That’s done,” he said, and went out of the door and away down the road, tap-tap-tapping into the distance.",
"The captain opened his hand and looked. It was a small round piece of paper, black on one side; on the other side somebody had written: TEN O’CLOCK.",
"“Ten o’clock!” he cried. “Six hours. We’ll do them yet!” And he sprang to his feet — and staggered — and put his hand to his throat, and fell his full length on the floor.",
"He was dead.",
"I called my mother, and we told each other everything we knew, which was very little and very frightening. There was money owing to us; there were pirates coming at ten o’clock; and there was nobody in the village brave enough to come back with us.",
"So we went back alone, in the dark. We opened the captain’s chest and found clothes, a quadrant, two pistols, a bar of silver, some tropical shells, and at the bottom an oilskin packet and a canvas bag full of coins — English guineas, French louis d’ors, Spanish doubloons, coins from countries I had never heard of.",
"My mother sat down on the floor and began to count them, because she would take exactly what the captain owed her and not one penny more.",
"“Take it and let’s go,” I begged her.",
"“I am an honest woman,” she said, “and I will have my own and no more.”",
"And then, out in the frozen night, we heard it: tap — tap — tap, coming up the road.",
"I took the oilskin packet, my mother took what she had counted, and we ran out of the back and hid under the little bridge below the inn. From there we heard the pirates break in, and search, and shout at one another that the chest had been opened and the packet was gone. Then the blind man’s voice rose above all of them, screaming at them to find us.",
"They were still searching when we heard horses coming fast along the road. The pirates ran in every direction. Blind Pew, left alone in the middle of the road, tapped up and down calling for his comrades — and the first horse came over the rise and rode him down, and he did not move again.",
"The riders were the customs officers, and they had come for smugglers. They had found something else."
],
3:[
"Doctor Livesey and Squire Trelawney were sitting together by the fire at the squire’s house when the officer brought me in with the oilskin packet in my hands.",
"“Have you heard of this Flint?” asked the squire.",
"“Heard of him!” said the doctor. “He was the most bloodthirsty pirate that ever sailed. The Spanish were more afraid of him than they were of the devil himself.”",
"“And the treasure?” said the squire. “Every seaman in Bristol has heard of it, and nobody has ever found it. If these papers tell us where it is—”",
"“If they do,” said the doctor, “then we will fit out a ship, and take Hawkins here as cabin boy, and you shall be a rich man till you die.”",
"They cut the seals with great care, and out of the packet fell two things: a small notebook full of Flint’s accounts, and a sealed paper.",
"When the doctor opened the paper, it was a map — the map of an island, about nine miles long and five across, with two harbours and a hill in the middle marked ‘The Spy-glass’. There were three crosses in red ink, and beside the one in the south-west, in a small neat hand very different from the captain’s, were the words: BULK OF TREASURE HERE.",
"On the back somebody had written directions:",
"“Tall tree, Spy-glass shoulder, bearing a point to the N. of N.N.E. Skeleton Island E.S.E. and by E. Ten feet.”",
"The squire jumped up and walked about the room. “Livesey,” he said, “you will give up that miserable practice of yours at once. Tomorrow I start for Bristol. In three weeks — two weeks — ten days! — we shall have the best ship, sir, and the choicest crew in England.”",
"“Trelawney,” said the doctor, “I’ll go with you. Jim will go with us. And I’ll answer for the two of us. There’s only one man I’m afraid of.”",
"“And who is that?” cried the squire. “Name the dog, sir!”",
"“You,” replied the doctor, “for you cannot hold your tongue. We are not the only men who know of this paper. Those fellows who attacked the inn tonight want that map, and they will stop at nothing to get it. Not one of us must breathe a word of what we have found.”",
"“Livesey,” said the squire, “you are always right. I shall be as silent as the grave.”",
"He was not silent as the grave. He was not silent for a week.",
"I know now, from what we learned later, exactly what happened in Bristol. The squire talked to the men who sold him the ship; he talked to the sailmakers and the ropemakers; he talked in the taverns along the docks about the fortune he was going to bring home. And in Bristol, in those days, half the men who had ever sailed with Flint were living quietly ashore, waiting for something exactly like this.",
"He wrote to us that he had bought a schooner called the Hispaniola, and that a one-legged old sailor called Long John Silver, who kept an inn near the docks, had helped him to find a crew of proper seamen. He was very pleased about the crew.",
"So it was arranged. In the last week of the month I said goodbye to my mother, and to the cove where I had lived all my life, and to the old Admiral Benbow with its new sign and its new landlord.",
"I remember that I looked back at it from the top of the hill, and that it seemed very small and grey in the morning light, and that I was suddenly and unexpectedly sorry."
],
4:[
"In Bristol the squire gave me a note and sent me down to the docks to find the Spy-glass Inn.",
"It was a bright, cheerful little place. The customers were mostly seafaring men, and they talked so loudly that I stopped at the door, half afraid to go in.",
"Then a man came out of a side room, and I knew at once that he was Long John Silver. His left leg was cut off close by the hip, and under his left shoulder he carried a crutch, which he managed with wonderful skill, hopping about upon it like a bird. He was very tall and strong, with a face as big as a ham — plain and pale, but intelligent and smiling.",
"I confess I was afraid of the one-legged sailor of my dreams for about five seconds. Then he shook my hand, and I was ashamed of myself. There was nothing frightening about this man at all: he was clean, and cheerful, and he spoke to me as if I were a grown man.",
"And then I looked past him at a customer near the door — a pale man with two fingers missing.",
"“Oh!” I cried. “Stop him! It’s Black Dog!”",
"“I don’t care two coppers who he is,” cried Silver, “but he hasn’t paid his score. Harry, run and catch him!”",
"There was a great deal of shouting and running, and three men went out after him, and Silver stumped up and down on his crutch, calling out orders and swearing that he would have the man back.",
"“Black Dog?” he said to me when they came back empty-handed. “I never heard the name. But I’ve seen him here before, I think, with a blind beggar.” He looked so honest and so angry that I would have trusted him with my life. And that is exactly the point.",
"A few days later we went aboard the Hispaniola, and Captain Smollett came to the cabin to speak to the squire and the doctor.",
"“Sir,” he said, “I’ll speak plainly. I don’t like this cruise, I don’t like the men, and I don’t like my officer. That’s short and sweet.”",
"“Perhaps, sir, you don’t like the ship either?” said the squire, very angry.",
"“I’ve not seen her tried,” said the captain. “She seems a clever craft. But I’ll tell you what I do know. I was hired to sail this ship where the gentleman told me. Very good. Then I find that every man before the mast knows more than I do: that we are going after treasure, that there is a map with an island and crosses on it, and even the exact position of the island. That’s not how I like to do business.”",
"He asked for two things: that the powder and arms be moved under the cabin, where the loyal men slept, and that the map be kept secret from everybody. The squire was furious. The doctor said, quietly, “I think the captain is talking sense.”",
"So we sailed. And it must be said that Long John Silver was the best man on that ship. He was up before the others and last to bed; he mended clothes, told stories, sang, and kept his galley as clean as a new pin. Every sailor on board would have done anything for him, and so, very nearly, would I.",
"There was a barrel of apples standing open on the deck, so that any man could help himself. It was the squire’s idea, and it very nearly saved all our lives.",
"On the last evening before we sighted land, I wanted an apple. There were only a few left at the bottom, so I climbed into the barrel — and there, in the dark, with the ship rolling gently, I fell asleep.",
"I woke because a heavy man had sat down against the barrel with a bump, and the whole thing shook. I was about to jump up when I heard the first words, and then I lay perfectly still, shaking with fear and curiosity, because from those few words I understood that the lives of every honest man aboard depended upon me alone.",
"It was Long John Silver, and he was talking to one of the youngest sailors.",
"“I sailed with Flint,” he was saying. “I was quartermaster on his ship, and I lost my leg in the same broadside that took Pew’s eyes. Flint was afraid of nothing living — except me. Now, here’s the way of it. Some of them are gentlemen of fortune and some of them aren’t. I have my share of the trade, and I put it in a bank, and I live quiet.”",
"“And what about the others?” asked the boy. “The captain, and the doctor, and the squire?”",
"“We let them find the treasure first,” said Silver comfortably. “They’ll help us get it aboard. And then—”",
"“And then?”",
"“Dead men don’t bite,” said Silver, in exactly the same warm, kindly voice he had used to me every day of the voyage.",
"I lay in the barrel in the dark, and I understood that this man — who had called me his mate, who had told me stories, who had spoken to me like a friend — was planning to kill every person on board I cared about.",
"And then, from the mast above us, a voice shouted: “Land ho!”"
],
5:[
"That night, when the ship was quiet, I told the captain, the doctor and the squire everything I had heard.",
"“Jim,” said the doctor, “you have saved our lives, and we shall not forget it.”",
"Then they counted. There were nineteen men aboard whom we could not trust. Against them we had seven: the captain, the doctor, the squire, Redruth, Hunter, Joyce — and me.",
"“We must wait,” said Captain Smollett. “We can do nothing until we know which of them is with us. And we must say nothing, because the moment they see that we know, the killing starts.”",
"In the morning we saw Treasure Island, and I have never forgotten my first sight of it. Grey woods covered most of it, with strips of yellow sand and tall trees that looked like broken pillars. Over the woods rose the Spy-glass, three or four hundred feet high, the strangest shape on the island. The whole place had a look of stillness that I disliked at once, and there was a smell of wet leaves and rotting wood that came out to us over the water.",
"There was no wind and a terrible heat. The men worked badly and answered slowly, and by the middle of the day the ship was full of a bad temper that anybody could feel.",
"Captain Smollett saw the danger, and he did a clever thing. He offered the men the afternoon ashore.",
"“If they come back late,” he said quietly to us, “I’ll fire a gun and set them adrift. If they don’t come back at all, so much the better.”",
"Thirteen of them went into the boats. And then I did the maddest thing I did in the whole business: as the boats pushed off I dropped over the side into the nearest one, and nobody said a word except one man, who looked at me strangely.",
"The moment the boat touched the sand I was out of it and away, running into the trees, with Silver’s voice calling my name behind me.",
"For half an hour I was perfectly happy. The island was strange and full of things I had never seen — flowers I did not know the names of, a snake that lifted its head and hissed at me, birds in the marsh. I forgot the pirates completely.",
"Then I heard voices, and I crept forward through the bushes on my hands and knees until I could see.",
"Long John Silver was standing in the marsh with a sailor called Tom. Silver had thrown his hat on the ground, and he was speaking earnestly, almost affectionately, and putting his hand on the other man’s arm.",
"“Tom,” he was saying, “it’s because I think gold dust of you that I’m warning you. If one of the wild ones knew what I was telling you now, where would I be? Come, now — you’re a good man, and there’s not a better in this crew. Join us.”",
"“Silver,” said Tom, “you’re old and you’re honest, or has the name for it. And you’ve money, which lots of poor sailors haven’t. And you’re brave. Am I to believe that you’d let yourself be led away with that kind of a mess of swabs? Not I! If I die like a dog, I’ll die doing my duty.”",
"And at that moment a cry came out of the marsh far away — a long, horrible cry, and then another, and then silence.",
"“What was that?” said Tom.",
"“That?” said Silver, and his face did not change at all. “Oh, I reckon that’ll be Alan.”",
"“Alan!” cried Tom. “Then rest his soul for a true seaman. As for you, John Silver — you’ve been a mate of mine, but you’re a mate of mine no more. If I die like a dog, I’ll die in my duty. You’ve killed Alan, have you? Then kill me too, if you can.”",
"And with that the brave fellow turned his back on the cook and set off walking towards the beach.",
"Silver did not move for a moment. Then he grabbed a branch, whipped the crutch out from under his arm, and sent it hurtling through the air. It struck poor Tom point foremost between the shoulders, and he fell face down on the grass.",
"Whether he was badly hurt or not, none can tell. Silver, active as a monkey even without leg or crutch, was on the top of him next moment, and had twice buried his knife up to the hilt in that defenceless body.",
"I do not know what it is to faint, but I know that for the next little while the whole world swam away from me in a whirling mist. When I came to myself, Silver was on his feet again, cleaning his knife upon a wisp of grass, and looking about him as calmly as a man who has finished a piece of work.",
"Then he took a whistle from his pocket and blew several loud notes, and I understood that other men were coming, and that if they found me they would do to me what had been done to Tom.",
"I got out of that thicket and ran as I have never run before or since."
],
6:[
"I ran until I could not run any more, and when I finally stopped it was in a strange part of the island, on the lower slopes of the Spy-glass, among rocks and small trees.",
"And then, out of the corner of my eye, I saw something move on the hillside.",
"I could not tell what it was — a bear, a man, or a monkey. It was dark and shaggy, and it ran from tree-trunk to tree-trunk, doubled up almost to the ground, but moving like a deer on two legs. It was coming towards me.",
"Now I was caught between two dangers, and the strange thing is how quickly I chose. Behind me were the murderers; in front of me was this unknown creature. And I found that I would rather face a wild man than Long John Silver, so I turned and began to walk towards it.",
"The figure stopped, hesitated, came forward — and then, to my astonishment, dropped on its knees and held out its clasped hands.",
"“Who are you?” I asked.",
"“Ben Gunn,” he answered, and his voice sounded hoarse and awkward, like a rusty lock. “I’m poor Ben Gunn, I am; and I haven’t spoke with a Christian these three years.”",
"He was a white man like myself, but the sun had burned him almost black, and his eyes looked very strange in that dark face. His clothes were the most remarkable thing about him: they were made of goatskins and old ship’s canvas, held together with brass buttons, bits of stick and loops of tarry rope.",
"“Three years!” I cried. “Were you shipwrecked?”",
"“Nay, mate,” said he. “Marooned.”",
"I had heard the word, and I knew it meant a horrible kind of punishment: a man put ashore on an empty coast, with a little powder and shot, and left there.",
"“Marooned three years agone,” he continued, “and lived on goats since then, and berries, and oysters. Wherever a man is, says I, a man can do for himself. But, mate, my heart is sore for Christian diet. You mightn’t happen to have a piece of cheese about you, now? No? Well, many’s the long night I’ve dreamed of cheese — toasted, mostly — and woke up again, and here I were.”",
"“If ever I get aboard again,” said I, “you shall have cheese by the pound.”",
"He looked at me with such hope that it was painful, and then he began, in his broken way, to ask me questions: what ship I came in, who the captain was, and — again and again — whether I was sure that this was not Flint’s ship.",
"When I told him that Flint was dead, but that some of Flint’s men were aboard, he went grey under his sunburn.",
"“Not — not a man with one leg?” he asked.",
"“Silver?” said I.",
"“Ah, Silver!” said he. “That were his name.”",
"So I told him our whole story, and he listened to every word as though it were the best thing he had ever heard.",
"“You’re a good lad, Jim,” he said when I had finished, “and you’re all in a clove hitch, aren’t you? Well, you just put your trust in Ben Gunn. Would your squire prove a generous-minded gentleman, do you think, if he were helped — say, by a man in trouble?”",
"I told him the squire was the most generous of men.",
"“Ay, but I don’t mean giving me a gate to keep and a suit of livery clothes,” said he. “I mean, would he be likely to come down to, say, one thousand pounds out of money that’s as good as a man’s own already?”",
"“I am sure he would,” said I. “As it was, all hands were to share.”",
"“And a passage home?” he added, watching me closely.",
"“Of course,” I said. “The squire is a gentleman. And besides, if we got rid of the others, we should need your help to work the ship home.”",
"“So you would,” he said, and seemed very much relieved.",
"Then he took my arm, and lowered his voice, and told me the strangest thing of all.",
"“I were in Flint’s ship when he buried the treasure,” he said. “He and six strong seamen. They was ashore nigh on a week, and us standing off and on in the old Walrus. One fine day up went the signal, and here come Flint by himself in a little boat, and his head done up in a blue scarf. The sun was getting up, and mortal white he looked about the face. But there he were, you mind, and the six all dead — dead and buried. How he done it, not a man aboard us could make out.”",
"“And afterwards?” I asked.",
"“Well,” said he, “three years ago I were in another ship, and we sighted this island. ‘Boys,’ said I, ‘here’s Flint’s treasure; let’s land and find it.’ Twelve days we looked for it, and every day they had the worse word for me. Then, one fine morning, they all went aboard. ‘As for you, Benjamin Gunn,’ says they, ‘here’s a musket, and a spade, and a pick-axe. You can stay here and find Flint’s money for yourself.’ Well, Jim, three years have I been here, and not a bite of Christian diet from that day to this.”",
"He winked at me and pinched my arm.",
"“But I weren’t idle, Jim,” he said. “I weren’t idle at all. Just you tell your squire that, and remember what I said about the passage and the share. And mark this, Jim: Ben Gunn is a rich man. A rich man.”",
"I thought that three years alone had turned his head, and I said nothing. And that was the biggest mistake I made on Treasure Island, though I did not learn it for many weeks.",
"Then, from far away over the trees, came the sound of a cannon."
],
7:[
"What follows I did not see, and Doctor Livesey has written it down for me.",
"While Jim was ashore, we found ourselves in a difficult position. Seven men cannot hold a ship against nineteen, and the Hispaniola had become a trap. Hunter and I went ashore in the little boat to look at the ground, and we found the stockade.",
"It was a strong log house on a small hill, big enough for forty people, with loopholes cut for muskets on every side, and — best of all — a spring of clear water inside the fence. A ship’s company could hold that place against a regiment, so long as they had food.",
"We decided to abandon the ship. We loaded the boat with powder, muskets, bags of biscuit, kegs of pork and my medicine chest, and we made two trips through the afternoon.",
"On the second trip the pirates saw what we were doing. One of them ran to the cannon on the deck of the Hispaniola, and they fired at us as we crossed the open water. The boat was overloaded and low in the water, and when the round shot came near, she filled and sank in three feet of water. We waded ashore with our arms above our heads, but we lost most of the powder and nearly all the food.",
"Poor Redruth, the squire’s old gamekeeper, was shot before we reached the stockade. He died an hour later, and we buried him in the sand and stood around him bareheaded in the breeze.",
"Then Captain Smollett brought a long roll of cloth out of the boat, and I saw what he meant to do.",
"“Captain,” said the squire, “are you sure? If we fly the flag, they will know exactly where we are.”",
"“Let them,” said Captain Smollett. “We are the King’s men here, and they are pirates. I will not take down the colours to please them.”",
"So the English flag went up over the log house, and it stayed there.",
"The pirates fired at it all afternoon. They wasted their powder on a piece of cloth, and the captain stood in the doorway and laughed at them and told them so, and I understood that he was doing it deliberately — that every hour they spent shooting at a flag was an hour they did not spend shooting at us.",
"The next morning a man came up the hill towards us with a white cloth on a stick. It was Long John Silver.",
"He struggled up the slope on his crutch and stopped below the fence.",
"“Flag of truce,” he called. “Cap’n Silver, sir, to come on board and make terms.”",
"“Cap’n Silver? I don’t know him,” said Captain Smollett. “Who’s he?”",
"“Me, sir. These poor lads have chosen me cap’n, after your desertion, sir.” He said the word ‘desertion’ with a great deal of pleasure. “We want that treasure, and we’ll have it. You give us the chart, and we’ll let you choose: come aboard with us when the treasure’s shipped, and I’ll give you my word to put you somewhere safe ashore. Or, if you like it better, stay here — and we’ll divide the stores with you, man for man.”",
"“Is that all?” said the captain.",
"“Every last word,” said Silver.",
"“Very good,” said Captain Smollett. “Now you’ll hear me. You can’t find the treasure. You can’t sail the ship — there’s not a man among you fit to sail her. You can’t fight us. And you’re in a bad way, Mr Silver: worse than you know. If you come up one at a time, unarmed, I’ll clap you all in irons and take you home to a fair trial in England. That’s my offer, and it’s the last good word you’ll get from me.”",
"Silver’s face was a picture. His eyes started in his head with wrath.",
"“Give me a hand up,” he cried.",
"“Not I,” said the captain.",
"“Who’ll give me a hand up?” he roared.",
"Not a man among us moved. He crawled along the sand until he could get hold of the fence, and pulled himself upright on his crutch, and spat into the spring.",
"“There!” he cried. “That’s what I think of ye. Before an hour’s out, I’ll stove in your old block house like a rum puncheon. Them that die’ll be the lucky ones.”",
"An hour later they came. They came out of the woods on the north side, shouting, and there was a volley from the trees, and then the boarding party ran at the fence and swarmed over it like monkeys.",
"For a few minutes there was smoke, and shouting, and the sound of blows inside the house, and I could not tell you the order of anything that happened. When it cleared, five of them were down and the rest were running for the woods. But Joyce was shot dead at his loophole, Hunter was dying, and Captain Smollett was badly wounded in two places.",
"We had beaten them. And there were four of us left who could still stand."
],
8:[
"I lay awake that afternoon in the stockade with a plan in my head that I knew perfectly well I ought not to carry out.",
"Ben Gunn had told me about a boat he had made — a little home-made coracle of goatskin stretched over a frame of wood, hidden under a tent of goatskins on the white rock at the far end of the anchorage. If I could get to it after dark, I could reach the Hispaniola, cut her adrift, and let the tide take her where it would. Then the pirates would have no ship at all.",
"It was a good plan, and it was not mine to make. I said nothing to anybody. I filled my pockets with biscuit, took a brace of pistols, and slipped out of the stockade while the doctor and the squire were busy with the captain’s wounds.",
"It was quite dark when I found the coracle. It was the strangest boat I have ever seen: about five and a half feet long, lopsided, and so light that I could carry it easily. It also had, as I discovered, the wickedest habit of any boat afloat — it would go anywhere except where you wanted it to go.",
"The night was black. The pirates aboard the Hispaniola had a great fire on the beach and another in the cabin, and I could hear them singing, over and over, a song with no end to it.",
"I paddled out until I felt the hawser under my hand: the great rope that held the ship to her anchor. It was as taut as a bowstring, and I understood that if I cut it while it was tight, the ship would go through the water like a kicking horse and might take me down with her.",
"So I waited, and I was lucky. A breeze came off the shore and the ship swung, and the hawser went slack in my hand, and I cut it — first one strand, then another, until only two were left, and then I waited again for the next breeze.",
"While I waited, I heard voices in the cabin above me: two men, and they were quarrelling. And then the Hispaniola swung slowly round, and the last strands parted, and she was adrift.",
"So was I. The ebb-tide had begun, and it took the ship and my little coracle together and carried us both out of the anchorage towards the open sea.",
"I could not steer. I discovered very quickly that the only thing to do with that boat was to lie still in the bottom of it and let it ride the waves as it liked, and that if I moved she would tip me into the water.",
"So I lay there in the dark, soaked with spray, watching the fire on the beach get smaller, hearing the surf on the rocks, and I was more frightened than I have ever been in my life. Some time before dawn I fell asleep from pure exhaustion, and dreamed of home.",
"When I woke, the sun was up, and I was at the south-west end of the island, tossing in the swell. And there, not half a mile from me, was the Hispaniola.",
"All her sails were set, and she was moving — slowly, awkwardly, first one way and then the other, like a ship with a drunk man at the wheel. And when I looked more carefully I saw that there was nobody at the wheel at all.",
"I paddled towards her. Every time she swung she came nearer, and the sea was going down, and at last, when her bow came round and stood over my little boat, I stood up and jumped and caught the jib-boom and pulled myself aboard.",
"And as I came down onto the deck, the coracle went under the ship’s side and was gone, and I was on the Hispaniola, alone — or nearly alone."
],
9:[
"There were two men on the deck, and both of them were down.",
"One lay on his back, stiff as a piece of wood, with his arms out. The other was Israel Hands, the coxswain, sitting against the bulwarks with his chin on his chest, his face as white as a candle, and a great stain of blood on the planks beside him. There was an empty brandy bottle rolling about with the movement of the ship.",
"He opened his eyes and looked at me for a long time before he spoke.",
"“Brandy,” he said.",
"I went below and brought him a little, and food, and cloth for his leg, and while he ate and drank the colour came back into his face a little.",
"“Who are you?” he said at last.",
"“I’ve come aboard to take possession of this ship, Mr Hands,” I said, as steadily as I could, “and you’ll please regard me as your captain until further notice.”",
"He looked at me sourly but said nothing. Then I did the thing I had been wanting to do since I came over the side: I hauled down the black flag and threw it overboard.",
"“God save the King!” said I, waving my cap. “And there’s an end to Captain Silver!”",
"He watched me with his chin on his breast, and he was clever enough to say nothing at all for a while.",
"“Cap’n Hawkins,” he said finally, in a friendly, reasonable way, “I reckon you’ll want to get ashore, and I reckon you can’t sail this ship alone. Now, you give me food and drink and a bit of silk to tie my wound up, and I’ll tell you how to sail her. Is that a bargain?”",
"It was a bargain, and neither of us meant to keep it. I knew that. He knew that I knew it. And for the next three hours we were the politest pair of enemies that ever sailed a ship together.",
"He told me how to bring her round; I hauled on the ropes and did what he said, and slowly, awkwardly, the Hispaniola came into the northern inlet where we could run her up on the sand.",
"He was very good company. He called me “Cap’n Hawkins” and told me stories about the sea, and asked me questions about my home, and thanked me for the brandy. And all the time his eyes followed me around the deck, and every time I turned my back I felt them.",
"When we were nearly in, he asked me to go below and bring him a bottle of wine.",
"“The brandy’s too strong for my head,” he said.",
"I said I would. And then I did not go below at all. I ran forward, slipped down the fore hatch, and came up again by a crack in the deck planking where I could see the whole of the after part of the ship.",
"Israel Hands had got up on his hands and knees. He crossed the deck quickly — his wounded leg giving him no trouble whatever — took a long knife out of a coil of rope by the scuppers, tried the point on his hand, and hid it inside his jacket. Then he went back to his place against the bulwarks and groaned.",
"So now I knew. He could move. He was armed. And when the ship touched the sand, he meant to kill me.",
"I came back with the wine and we drank each other’s health, and he was more friendly than ever.",
"We ran her in. The bow struck the sand and the ship heeled over sharply, and I lost my footing on the sloping deck — and when I looked up, Hands was already halfway to me with the dirk in his hand.",
"I ran. I went up the shrouds hand over hand and did not stop until I was sitting on the crosstrees, with my two pistols out and Hands climbing slowly after me.",
"“One more step, Mr Hands,” said I, “and I’ll blow your brains out. Dead men don’t bite, you know.”",
"He stopped at once. He was too slow-witted to hide his disappointment.",
"“Jim,” he said, “I reckon you and me will have to come to terms.”",
"And then, so quickly that I hardly saw him move, his right hand went back and something sang through the air like an arrow. I felt a blow and a sharp pain, and I found that my shoulder was pinned to the mast.",
"In the horrid pain and surprise of the moment — I am not sure that I meant to do it, and I know that I did not aim — both my pistols went off, and both fell out of my hands. They did not fall alone. With a choked cry, the coxswain loosed his grasp upon the shrouds and plunged head first into the water.",
"I did not feel brave. I did not feel proud. I sat on the crosstrees with the blood running down my back, shaking all over, and the only thing I wanted in the world was for it not to have happened."
],
10:[
"By the time I got ashore it was dark, and I set off across the island towards the stockade in a very good opinion of myself. I had taken the ship. I had beaten Israel Hands. Now I would walk in and tell them.",
"I saw the light of a fire through the trees and came up quietly, meaning to surprise my friends.",
"And a shrill voice broke out of the darkness: “Pieces of eight! Pieces of eight! Pieces of eight!”",
"It was Silver’s parrot, Captain Flint. Somebody threw a torch on the fire and it flared up, and I saw six men lying around it — and Long John Silver standing in the middle of them, looking straight at me.",
"“So,” said he, “here’s Jim Hawkins, shiver my timbers! Dropped in, like, eh? Well, I take that friendly.”",
"He sat down on a brandy cask and began to fill his pipe as if nothing had happened, and told me the news: my friends had left the stockade, and the pirates had it now.",
"One of the men — a man with a bandaged head — stood up with his knife out. “We’ll have no more of this,” he said. “Let me finish the boy.”",
"“Would you, now?” said Silver, and he did not raise his voice at all. “Him that wants to speak to John Silver had better hurry up, for I’ll have his heart out and see the colour of it before that pipe’s empty. Sit down.”",
"The man sat down.",
"Later, when the others had gone outside to argue among themselves, Silver spoke to me quietly.",
"“Now, Jim,” he said, “you’re within half a plank of death — and, what’s a long sight worse, of torture. They want to be rid of you. But you mark me: I’m on your side now, and here’s the reason. I heard you saying you had taken the ship. Well, if I keep you alive, I’ve got a witness. When it comes to a trial in England — and it will come to a trial — you’ll speak for John Silver, and John Silver will have saved your life. That’s a fair trade, Jim. Is it a bargain?”",
"It was not friendship, and he did not pretend it was. It was business. But he had put himself between me and six knives, and I said yes, and I gave him my word.",
"In the morning the crew came back from the woods together, and one of them walked forward and put something into Silver’s hand.",
"It was a piece of paper, round, and blackened on one side; on the other side was written one word: DEPOSED.",
"Silver looked at it, turned it over, and laughed.",
"“Where might you have got the paper?” he asked. “Why — hullo! Look here, now, this ain’t lucky. You’ve gone and cut this out of a Bible. What fool’s cut a Bible?”",
"The men stared at each other. There is no more superstitious set of people in the world than sailors, and the sight of that torn page frightened them far more than the black spot itself. In two minutes, Silver had turned their own judgement into a joke against them, and they were his men again.",
"That morning Doctor Livesey came up the hill with his bag in his hand, and Silver walked out to meet him under a truce.",
"The doctor treated every wounded pirate in that camp, one after another, exactly as if they had been paying patients in an English village. He never once mentioned what they had done.",
"Then he asked to speak to me alone, and we stood by the fence.",
"“Jim,” he said, “run for it. Come with me now, and we’ll be gone before they can move.”",
"“I can’t,” I said. “I gave my word.”",
"He was silent for a moment, and I could see how much it cost him.",
"“Well,” he said at last, “then keep it.”"
],
11:[
"In the morning we set out for the treasure: six pirates, Silver and me. Silver had a rope round my waist and the end of it in his hand, and I walked in front of him all that day like a bear at a fair.",
"We followed the directions on the map. It was a bright, hot morning, and the men were in high spirits, laughing and talking about what they would do with the money.",
"Then one of them, walking ahead, gave a cry.",
"At the foot of a tall pine, under a green creeper, lay a human skeleton, with a few rags of clothing on the ground. It was lying perfectly straight — feet pointing one way, hands raised above the head like a diver, pointing the other.",
"“He’s pointing,” said Silver, and there was something in his voice I had not heard before. “That’s Flint’s work. Six of them he took ashore, and six he killed. And then he laid one of them out along the line of the treasure, for a compass. Ay — that’s Flint, sure enough.”",
"The men were silent after that, and they walked closer together.",
"We came up onto the shoulder of the hill, and the man in front sat down suddenly and would not go on. And then, out of the trees in front of us, thin and high and trembling, came a voice singing:",
"“Fifteen men on the dead man’s chest — Yo-ho-ho and a bottle of rum!”",
"I have never seen men more frightened than those pirates. The colour went out of six faces at once. Some got to their feet, some clung to each other.",
"“It’s Flint,” said one, in a whisper.",
"“Come,” said Silver, and his lips were grey, but he spoke firmly. “Nobody in this world has ever seen a ghost with an echo. Ghosts don’t make a shadow neither, and they don’t sing out of tune. That voice is a man’s voice — and I’ve heard it before.” He listened again, and then he began to laugh. “Ben Gunn!” he said. “Ben Gunn, by all that’s wonderful.”",
"It steadied them, and we went on.",
"And so we came at last to the tall tree, and to the place where the treasure was buried.",
"There was a great hole in the ground, not freshly made — the sides had fallen in and grass had grown in the bottom. In it lay the broken handle of a pickaxe and the boards of several packing cases.",
"The treasure was gone. Seven hundred thousand pounds, and it had been gone for years.",
"For one moment there was silence. Then the men jumped down into the hole and began to dig with their fingers, throwing the boards aside, and then they turned round and looked at Long John Silver with murder in their faces.",
"And at that moment three shots came out of the thicket. Two of the men fell, and the rest ran; and out of the trees came Doctor Livesey, Abraham Gray — and Ben Gunn, with his musket smoking in his hands.",
"“Thank ye kindly, doctor,” said Silver, quite calmly. “You came in about the nick, I guess, for me and Hawkins.”",
"Afterwards they told me the whole of it. Ben Gunn, in his long lonely wanderings, had found the skeleton and the treasure two years before. He had dug it up with his own hands, and carried it on his back, journey after journey, across the island to a cave on the north side, where it had been lying safely ever since.",
"That was why the doctor had given Silver the map without a fight: because the map was worthless, and he knew it.",
"It took us three days to carry the gold to the ship. It was the strangest collection of money I have ever seen: English guineas, French louis, Spanish doubloons and pieces of eight, coins from India and China with strange writing on them, coins with pictures of kings who had been dead two hundred years. I was kept busy all day packing minted money into bread-bags, and I was tired to death of it before the end.",
"We left three of the pirates on the island, with food, tools, powder and medicine, because we could not carry them home to be hanged.",
"And at a port in Spanish America, where we stopped for water, Long John Silver went ashore in the night with one of the bags of coins — about four hundred guineas — and was never seen again by any of us.",
"I was glad to be rid of him so cheaply.",
"Every man of us had a share of the treasure, and used it wisely or foolishly according to his nature. Ben Gunn got a thousand pounds and spent it in nineteen days, which was exactly what everybody had expected. Captain Smollett has retired from the sea.",
"As for me, I have my share, and I shall never go back to that accursed island. The worst dreams I have are when I hear the surf booming on its coasts, or start upright in bed with the sharp voice of Captain Flint still ringing in my ears: “Pieces of eight! Pieces of eight!”"
],
};

